nsensus at Lean R despite one Tossup rating", "Killin outraised Crank and a July D-sponsored poll showed a one-point race", "Thin, dated polling (one usable poll, 65 days old) keeps uncertainty high"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point to a narrow Republican edge: a five-point partisan lean plus incumbency, offset partly by a Democratic-leaning environment and Killin's strong fundraising. The lone recent poll shows a one-point race, and markets price Crank near 69 percent, so a Lean R forecast with a modest Republican margin fits the evidence.", "watch": "Fresh independent polling or a shift in the national environment could move this toward Tossup, since the only public poll already showed a statistical tie.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Newest usable poll is 65 days old; polling uncertainty inflated.", "Incumbent Jeff Crank (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Colorado's 5th District, anchored in Colorado Springs, has historically favored Republicans, but first-term incumbent Jeff Crank faces a well-funded challenge from Democrat Jessica Killin in what raters treat as a competitive race. Most analysts call it Lean R, though one rates it a Tossup and another Tilt R, and prediction markets give Crank roughly a two-in-three chance.\n\nKillin has actually out-raised Crank, and a Democratic-sponsored July poll showed the race at a single point, fueling talk that a Democrat could win this seat for the first time. Still, the district's five-point Republican lean, Crank's incumbency, and outside spending defending him give the GOP the edge. With only one usable poll and it now two months old, uncertainty remains wide, and the race sits closer to the middle than most Republican-held seats.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.332, "margin": -2.57}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.3239, "margin": -3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.3532, "margin": -3.0}]}